          "distinct": true,
          "id": "8852a47a725ce7b3fd7abfee6353f28691f46235",
          "message": "refactor(velvet): reverse the core's edges into Routing and Async's edge into the work loop (#1706)\n\nCloses #1657. Two dependencies now point from the outer parts toward the core instead of from the core outward:\n- the core reconciler and hook slots no longer name Routing types;\n- Async no longer names the work loop.\n\nThere is no public API change and no behaviour change.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **Routing registers with the core.**\n  - `HookBlockerSlot` (`Hooks/HookSlots.cs`) is now abstract: `LastDeps`, `NextDeps`, `Commit()`, `Dispose()`. The routing state moves to `Routing/RouteBlockerSlot.cs`. `FiberHookCommit.CommitBlockerSlots` calls `slot.Commit()`.\n  - `FiberOutletScope` holds an `IDisposable`. `SyncRenderingOutletScope<TState>` takes the state and a `Func<TState, IDisposable?>`. `RouteOutlet` passes the route and a cached static lambda, so it adds no per-render closure.\n- **Async owns its await gate.**\n  - `Async/AwaitContinuationGate.cs` holds the open-scope count, the deferral flag and the continuation queue.\n  - `FiberWorkLoop` updates the gate where it opens and closes transition scopes, and drains the gate's queue.\n  - `VelvetTask` and `VelvetMainThread` read only the gate.\n- **Guard: `CoreLayeringTests`.**\n  - The first case reads Routing's type names from the Routing sources and fails if `Reconciler/*` or `Hooks/HookSlots.cs` names one.\n  - The second fails if an Async source names `FiberWorkLoop`.\n  - Comments and strings are blanked first. Each case also fails when its scan finds too few names or sources.\n\n`Hooks.cs` and `V.cs` still name Routing types, because their public signatures do: `UseBlocker`, `RouterProvider` and `Route`. That is the 4.0 assembly split's question (#1644).\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **Red on `main`.** The same scan, emulated over `main`, names exactly the edges #1657 lists: `HookSlots.cs`, `FiberHookCommit.cs` and `FiberOutletScope.cs` for Routing, and `VelvetTask.cs` and `VelvetMainThread.cs` for the work loop.\n- **The test turns red when an edge comes back.** Adding `typeof(RouteBlockerManager)` to `FiberHookCommit.cs`, and a `FiberWorkLoop` call to `VelvetTask.cs`, reddens each case.\n- **Targeted EditMode run.** Routing, Async, transition and blocker fixtures: 1,222 cases, 0 failed and 0 inconclusive. `compile_check.py` passes on every assembly.\n\nNo CHANGELOG fragment: the change is internal.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
