          "distinct": true,
          "id": "38b3243c172e60b092683a43b2592d04e03e46d7",
          "message": "fix(velvet): stack a user wrapper by its element's z, and defer a hidden FocusScope's autofocus to the reveal (#1779)\n\nCloses #1567. Closes #1565.\n\n## #1567: a user `WrapElement`'s wrapper takes the element's z\n\nThe stacking facts for an element built with a user `WrapElement` are recorded under its wrapper, the box that stacks among the siblings. `FiberZLayerCoordinator.SlotOf` mapped only clip-path wrappers, so the out-of-pass readers (`OnResolved` for a `hover:z-*`, `Restack` for a `z-(--z)` whose property arrives later, `Reorder`, `OnPositionToggled`) resolved to the inner element: a `hover:z-*` moved the inner element inside its wrapper, and a custom-property `z-*` found no facts and did nothing. `WrapperInfrastructure.OuterOf` is now the one predicate both `SlotOf` and the patch path's `ResolveOuter` read.\n\n## #1565: a FocusScope under a hidden Suspense takes focus at the reveal\n\nA `FocusScope autoFocus` in a Portal that mounts under an already-hidden Suspense must not focus while `display: none` hides it; in React a hidden subtree's layout-phase work, a newly mounted host's autofocus included (`reappearLayoutEffects` → `commitHostMount`), runs when the subtree is revealed.\n\n- `FocusScopeDriver` defers the autofocus while the scope or its Portal's placeholder is hidden; `SuspenseOffscreenCommit` releases a deferred scope once both are visible.\n- The release runs inside the reveal commit. The revealed rows keep the computed `display: none` of their hide until the panel's next style pass, so the release applies the panel's styles first through `FiberLayoutReflow` (the path ref attachment already uses, with its guards against running inside the style traversal or the layout phase) and then focuses. Where that path cannot run, one attempt waits for the next tick.\n- Focus outside the scope does not stop the take, as with React's `autoFocus`.\n\n## A FocusScope added after mount never took focus\n\nCreated elements are inserted last to first, so a scope added to a mounted parent or to a live Portal range attached its `FocusScopeEnd` before its `FocusScopeStart`; the end's attach found no start and the autofocus never ran. Whichever marker attaches second now fires it, once.\n\n`focus.md` and the changelog state all three.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `ZIndexWrapElementSlotTests`: the hover case and the custom-property case each stack the wrapper among its siblings; both red on the base.\n- `SuspenseHiddenPortalFocusTests`:\n  - a scope added to a mounted parent, and one in a revealed Portal, take focus (red on the base: the end attached first);\n  - while hidden nothing holds focus and the autofocus is held, not dropped;\n  - at the reveal the button takes focus with no tick between, including after a style pass ran while hidden and with focus on a button outside the scope;\n  - a candidate a stylesheet hides is not focused, then or after a class change shows it;\n  - an unmounted scope, and a reveal of another boundary, focus nothing.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
