          "distinct": true,
          "id": "d7a28564f53e53f7ab627799b98180dcd912773d",
          "message": "fix(velvet): close the box-shadow and ring differences from Tailwind 4.3.3 left after the shadow reader rework (#1784)\n\nCloses #1568.\n\nCloses the box-shadow and ring differences from Tailwind 4.3.3 in Chrome that #1555 left, and the readers of a custom property in other families that share their mechanism. Expected values were read with `getComputedStyle` on Tailwind 4.3.3's compiled CSS in Chromium.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **An unresolved `var()` discards the box-shadow.** A shadow, inset-shadow or typed-length ring/inset-ring reader whose property resolves to nothing unsets to a value the discard reader rejects (`shadow-[0]`, `inset-shadow-[0]`, `ring-[length:0]`, `inset-ring-[length:2]`, where a bare `0` is a valid inset-ring length), and the readers skip a consumer's own raw token so it cannot override its payload. Before writing that unset on a panel the reader applies the panel's styles through `FiberLayoutReflow.TryLayOut` and reads the property again, so a property a stylesheet declares does not drop the ring before its first read; where styles cannot be applied there, it reads once more on the next tick.\n- **Which layers a colour class reaches.** Each layer is judged as Tailwind's `replace-shadow-colors` writes it, a `var()` layer by its source: a layer Tailwind writes no colour variable into carries `ShadowSpec.FixedColour`, which colour classes, `/N` and `-inherit` skip. A layer naming no colour is `currentcolor`; `ArbitraryDefaultColor` is gone.\n- **Payload placement.** `ComposePlacedClasses` compares a payload only with tokens of its own `StyleFamilyOrder` slot. An important reader's payload carries the bang and ranks among the important literals by Tailwind's order, for shadow, inset-shadow, ring, gap, skew, font and divide.\n- **Opacity modifiers.** `Normalize` reads a bracket followed by `/N` for the families Tailwind emits an opacity-modifier rule for, and `shadow-(--x)`. A colour root's bracket under a type hint other than its own width's is typed by its value, as Chrome does: a literal colour is drawn at N%, a literal non-colour leaves the earlier colour (a gradient stop goes transparent, a ring discards the box-shadow), and a `var()` is read as the colour it holds or, when it holds a length, a number, a word that names no colour or a colour past 100%, takes the property's unset. A ring's CSS-wide keyword stays undecided, as on main, except behind a modifier that reads no variable. `divide-` and gradient stops now read a modifier on a bracket or a palette name.\n- **Container units.** `cqw`, `cqi`, `cqh`, `cqb`, `cqmin` and `cqmax` resolve against the content width of the nearest `@container` ancestor (block-axis units against the panel), and against the panel where there is none, in every length bracket.\n- `font-[var(--w)]` no longer names a font family.\n- `styling-shadows.md`, `styling-custom-properties.md` and `styling-arbitrary-lengths.md` state the rules.\n\nColours Velvet's grammar does not read (CSS named colours outside its set, the deprecated system colours, colour functions) are #1282's.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `BoxShadowCustomPropertyTests`, `BoxShadowUnresolvedPanelTests` and `BoxShadowContainerUnitPanelTests` (new), each row with a declared-property or literal control: unresolved readers, the first read on mount and after a move between hosts, colour-class reach per layer, placement and importance, opacity modifiers per family and value kind, keywords in ring and shadow colour slots, and container units. Rows main gets wrong are red on the base; rows it already gets right are declared `GREEN_ON_BASE(characterization)`.\n- `RejectedValueTests`: `ring shadow-[0_0_var(--a)_var(--b)] shadow-[#ggg]` moves from \"discards\" to \"ring is drawn\", as Chrome draws it.\n- `StyleFontTests`: `font-[var(--w)]` and `font-(--w)` name no family.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
