          "distinct": true,
          "id": "f8208a158091c02d31073e5f17abee0df16160e3",
          "message": "test(velvet): flush state in tests through compiled delegates, so the re-render allocation benchmark counts only the reconciler (#1789)\n\nCloses #1650\n\n`UseState_RerenderClosureAlloc` drives each update through `FlushStateForTest`. That helper looked up the scheduler's four fields through `FieldInfo.GetValue`/`SetValue` on every call and boxed what it read. It also built a new `Action` for the `FlushState` method group each time. So the benchmark counted the helper's own allocations alongside the reconciler's.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **`FlushStateForTest`** now reads and writes those fields through delegates compiled once, and holds the method group in a static readonly delegate.\n- **No production member** was added.\n\n## Why this closes the issue\n\nMain already carries the issue's reconciler changes:\n\n- the cached drain callbacks;\n- the moved lambdas;\n- the pooled batch and drain lists;\n- the single-fiber ordering skip;\n- `ContextDependency` as a readonly struct.\n\n`FiberTreeReturn`'s per-ancestor closure is gone too. #1700's breakdown of the 22 blocks it left per update put 8 on this helper and 7 on `FiberTreeReturn`.\n\nThese kinds of allocation are still left:\n\n- the owed-settle window's list per flush;\n- `HookUpdateQueueSlot._baseQueue` rebuilt per `Process`, which `UndoCommit` holds by reference;\n- `FiberTreeTraversal.Visit`'s `Stack`.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `compile_check.py` passes over `Velvet.TestUtilities` and `Velvet.Tests.Reconciler.Editor`.\n- Nothing was measured locally. The benchmark's per-100-run count and times come from CI's benchmark data.\n\nChangelog: no fragment; only a test helper changes.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
          "timestamp": "2026-11-18T23:13:49Z",
